WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.967
A basketball is easy for
us to recognise, even

00:00:03.047 --> 00:00:05.593
when it is small,
blurred, or partly hidden.

00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:08.859
A detection model sees only
pixels and probabilities.

00:00:08.939 --> 00:00:12.479
It must learn which visual
patterns belong to the ball, where

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:15.957
the object begins and ends,
and how confident it should be.

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:19.570
Reco Trainer provides a local workflow for

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.619
teaching that model with
your own sports footage.

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:26.902
In this extended training, we will
follow the complete cycle: prepare

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:31.184
representative frames, review
labels, train locally, test on unseen

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:35.395
material, compare model revisions,
and decide what to improve next.

00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:39.357
Reco Trainer builds an
object detection model.

00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:41.652
For every image, the model predicts a

00:00:41.732 --> 00:00:44.318
location, a class, and a confidence score.

00:00:44.398 --> 00:00:46.392
A box answers where the object is.

00:00:46.472 --> 00:00:49.206
The class says what the
model thinks it found.

00:00:49.286 --> 00:00:54.019
Confidence describes how strongly the
visual evidence supports that guess.

00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:58.463
The model does not contain a
written rule for basketballs.

00:00:58.543 --> 00:01:01.133
It learns recurring
patterns from examples.

00:01:01.213 --> 00:01:04.366
A correct annotation is
therefore more than a drawing.

00:01:04.446 --> 00:01:06.333
It is the lesson we give the model.

00:01:07.413 --> 00:01:10.364
Reco Trainer starts with
RF-DETR, a general object

00:01:10.444 --> 00:01:13.818
detector that already contains
useful visual knowledge.

00:01:13.898 --> 00:01:16.990
Training adapts that knowledge
to your sport, camera

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.739
position, image quality,
and chosen classes.

00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:21.925
This process is called fine-tuning.

00:01:22.005 --> 00:01:25.520
It makes useful results possible
with hundreds of carefully

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.185
selected frames instead of
starting from millions of images.

00:01:30.265 --> 00:01:32.910
Reco Trainer follows a local-first design.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.855
Original videos remain
in the selected folder.

00:01:35.935 --> 00:01:40.200
Extracted frames, annotations,
training runs, model revisions, and

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.545
benchmark reports stay below the
local dot reco-training directory.

00:01:44.625 --> 00:01:47.417
The application has no
media upload function.

00:01:48.497 --> 00:01:52.188
An update check can ask whether a
newer Reco Trainer release exists.

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:55.892
That request does not include
project footage, labels, or models.

00:01:55.972 --> 00:01:59.795
Initial machine-learning setup
may download software dependencies

00:01:59.875 --> 00:02:03.500
and pretrained weights, but it
does not upload your sports videos.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:08.458
Local processing reduces the risk
of accidental disclosure, but it

00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:12.693
does not replace consent, copyright,
or data-protection obligations.

00:02:12.773 --> 00:02:15.888
You still need the appropriate
rights to record, train

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.804
on, and share anything
derived from the material.

00:02:18.884 --> 00:02:22.276
This demonstration uses only
approved synthetic footage.

00:02:23.356 --> 00:02:27.064
We begin by selecting a folder
that contains the training videos.

00:02:27.144 --> 00:02:30.163
Reco Trainer samples a
configurable number of images

00:02:30.243 --> 00:02:33.263
from each video and spreads
them across the duration.

00:02:33.343 --> 00:02:36.982
These extracted frames become
the raw material for annotation.

00:02:38.062 --> 00:02:42.039
The goal is useful variation, not
the largest possible frame count.

00:02:42.119 --> 00:02:45.882
Consecutive frames often contain
almost the same information.

00:02:45.962 --> 00:02:49.512
Fifty examples with different
distances, lighting, motion

00:02:49.592 --> 00:02:52.786
blur, occlusion, and camera
angles can teach more than

00:02:52.866 --> 00:02:56.131
hundreds of nearly identical
images from one sequence.

00:02:57.211 --> 00:02:59.760
The frame count applies
per video, so the total

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.053
grows with the size of the source folder.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:05.357
Start with a manageable set
that you can review carefully.

00:03:05.437 --> 00:03:08.122
You can remove an unsuitable
image from training

00:03:08.202 --> 00:03:10.549
without touching the original recording.

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:14.663
Reco Trainer deletes only the derived
frame and related dataset copies.

00:03:15.743 --> 00:03:18.864
When choosing material,
include the awkward cases.

00:03:18.944 --> 00:03:22.721
A fast ball near a player's hand,
a partial view at the edge of the

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:26.650
frame, or a dimly lit scene may
take longer to label, but those are

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.507
often the situations where the
final model needs the most help.

00:03:31.587 --> 00:03:34.586
Annotation is the most
important part of the workflow.

00:03:34.666 --> 00:03:36.795
A good box includes the complete object

00:03:36.875 --> 00:03:39.072
and stays close to its visible boundary.

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:42.486
A box that cuts through the ball
teaches an incomplete shape.

00:03:42.566 --> 00:03:46.636
A box with a large area of floor around
it teaches unnecessary background.

00:03:47.716 --> 00:03:50.167
Select the correct class before drawing.

00:03:50.247 --> 00:03:52.409
Then inspect every proposed object.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:55.230
Move or resize inaccurate
boxes, remove false

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.050
detections, and add
objects the model missed.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.232
Undo and redo keep the last
ten editing steps for each

00:04:01.312 --> 00:04:04.414
image, which makes detailed
correction less risky.

00:04:05.494 --> 00:04:09.186
Automatic labelling can save time
once a base model is available.

00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:11.586
It should always be treated as a proposal.

00:04:11.666 --> 00:04:14.397
Reco Trainer keeps
unreviewed candidates out of

00:04:14.477 --> 00:04:17.551
dataset generation, model
packages, and benchmarks.

00:04:17.631 --> 00:04:20.089
This prevents the model
from feeding its own

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:22.900
uncertain guesses directly
back into training.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.352
Consistency matters as much as accuracy.

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.804
Similar situations need similar labels.

00:04:28.884 --> 00:04:32.027
If one reviewer draws a tight
box and another includes a

00:04:32.107 --> 00:04:35.390
large margin, the model
receives conflicting examples.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.982
Agree on the meaning of
each class and review

00:04:38.062 --> 00:04:40.364
difficult cases with the same standard.

00:04:41.444 --> 00:04:44.423
Labelling can feel like
preparation for training, but in

00:04:44.503 --> 00:04:47.929
practical machine learning it is
the central teaching activity.

00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:50.160
The training process can only amplify the

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.837
information contained in
the reviewed examples.

00:04:52.917 --> 00:04:56.597
It cannot repair a dataset whose
answers are missing or inconsistent.

00:04:57.677 --> 00:05:00.574
After the first useful
model exists, Reco Trainer

00:05:00.654 --> 00:05:03.338
can help decide what
deserves attention next.

00:05:03.418 --> 00:05:05.748
This is the idea behind active learning.

00:05:05.828 --> 00:05:10.142
Review time goes first to examples
that are likely to teach something new.

00:05:11.222 --> 00:05:14.077
Low-confidence detections
indicate uncertainty.

00:05:14.157 --> 00:05:16.544
If two installed models
disagree, the frame

00:05:16.624 --> 00:05:18.745
probably contains genuine ambiguity.

00:05:18.825 --> 00:05:21.012
Temporal analysis adds another signal.

00:05:21.092 --> 00:05:24.014
When a detected ball jumps
to an implausible position

00:05:24.094 --> 00:05:27.081
between neighbouring frames,
the middle result may be

00:05:27.161 --> 00:05:30.082
a false detection rather
than an extraordinary pass.

00:05:31.162 --> 00:05:33.671
These signals only
change the review order.

00:05:33.751 --> 00:05:36.960
They never decide which answer
enters the training set.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.039
A person still confirms the
class, corrects the box,

00:05:40.119 --> 00:05:42.838
rejects the detection,
or skips the candidate.

00:05:42.918 --> 00:05:45.427
Active learning makes human attention more

00:05:45.507 --> 00:05:48.016
efficient without removing human control.

00:05:49.096 --> 00:05:53.357
Once the reviewed set contains enough
useful variation, we can train a model.

00:05:53.437 --> 00:05:56.962
An epoch means one complete pass
through the training examples.

00:05:57.042 --> 00:06:00.367
Reco Trainer uses one hundred
epochs as a practical default,

00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:03.372
but a larger number does not
guarantee a better model.

00:06:04.452 --> 00:06:06.940
With too little data,
a long training run can

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.062
memorise the exact training
images and their quirks.

00:06:10.142 --> 00:06:11.311
This is overfitting.

00:06:11.391 --> 00:06:14.086
The model may score well
on familiar footage and

00:06:14.166 --> 00:06:17.070
fail when the camera,
lighting, or players change.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:19.429
We therefore judge progress on material

00:06:19.509 --> 00:06:21.719
that the model never used for learning.

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:25.820
Normal training continues
from the current checkpoint.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.825
That is usually faster and preserves
what the active model already knows.

00:06:29.905 --> 00:06:33.119
Use Train from scratch when you
introduce a new class that the

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:36.414
existing classification head
was never trained to predict.

00:06:37.494 --> 00:06:39.597
Training stays on the local machine.

00:06:39.677 --> 00:06:44.245
Supported Apple systems use Metal
acceleration, while other configurations

00:06:44.325 --> 00:06:47.908
can use an available local
accelerator or fall back to the CPU.

00:06:47.988 --> 00:06:50.302
CPU training works, but it takes longer.

00:06:50.382 --> 00:06:54.035
During a tutorial recording, we
use a labelled time jump rather

00:06:54.115 --> 00:06:57.697
than pretending that a full
training run finishes instantly.

00:06:58.777 --> 00:07:02.614
The first model gives us a starting
point, not a finished detector.

00:07:02.694 --> 00:07:05.341
Review new videos applies
the active model to a

00:07:05.421 --> 00:07:08.349
separate folder and creates
a queue of candidates.

00:07:08.429 --> 00:07:11.146
Videos already used by
the project are skipped,

00:07:11.226 --> 00:07:13.384
so the review focuses on new material.

00:07:14.464 --> 00:07:17.374
Each candidate requires
a deliberate decision.

00:07:17.454 --> 00:07:20.291
Confirm the ball when
the detection is correct.

00:07:20.371 --> 00:07:22.260
Choose no ball for a false alarm.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:26.562
Correct the box when the object is
real but the position is inaccurate.

00:07:26.642 --> 00:07:29.333
Skip a case when it
cannot be judged reliably.

00:07:30.413 --> 00:07:33.335
Only reviewed candidates
become training evidence.

00:07:33.415 --> 00:07:36.270
After collecting a meaningful
batch, train another

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.939
revision and compare it
with the current model.

00:07:39.019 --> 00:07:42.608
This creates a controlled improvement
cycle: expose the model to

00:07:42.688 --> 00:07:46.344
new situations, examine its
mistakes, add verified examples, and

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:50.214
measure whether the next revision
handles those situations better.

00:07:51.294 --> 00:07:53.982
Avoid retraining after
every single correction.

00:07:54.062 --> 00:07:57.607
A coherent batch gives the training
run enough new information to

00:07:57.687 --> 00:08:01.100
justify the time and makes the
comparison easier to interpret.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:05.829
A fair test uses footage the model
has never seen during training.

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:08.812
Testing on familiar material
rewards memorisation

00:08:08.892 --> 00:08:11.050
and gives an overly optimistic result.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:15.389
The Independent model test creates a
held-out set for an honest comparison.

00:08:16.469 --> 00:08:19.875
Select a folder that has never
contributed training frames.

00:08:19.955 --> 00:08:22.815
Review the correct objects
carefully, then freeze

00:08:22.895 --> 00:08:25.413
those answers as the
benchmark ground truth.

00:08:25.493 --> 00:08:27.738
Every compatible model will receive the

00:08:27.818 --> 00:08:30.199
same images and the same expected answers.

00:08:31.279 --> 00:08:34.476
For this walkthrough, we use
one approved fifteen-second

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:37.018
synthetic clip to
illustrate the mechanics.

00:08:37.098 --> 00:08:39.693
That is enough to show the
workflow, but it is not

00:08:39.773 --> 00:08:42.770
enough to support a serious
claim about model quality.

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:45.980
A meaningful evaluation needs
much more independent and

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.859
representative footage across
different games, camera positions,

00:08:49.939 --> 00:08:53.805
lighting conditions, distances,
occlusions, and types of movement.

00:08:54.885 --> 00:08:56.374
Keep the test set separate.

00:08:56.454 --> 00:09:00.399
The moment a test frame influences
training decisions or becomes a new

00:09:00.479 --> 00:09:04.423
training example, it no longer
provides an independent examination.

00:09:04.503 --> 00:09:06.947
Replace contaminated test material before

00:09:07.027 --> 00:09:09.266
using the benchmark for a real decision.

00:09:10.346 --> 00:09:12.978
The local model comparison runs compatible

00:09:13.058 --> 00:09:15.690
revisions against the frozen ground truth.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:18.768
Models run one after another
so they do not compete

00:09:18.848 --> 00:09:21.627
for the same processor,
GPU, or shared memory.

00:09:22.707 --> 00:09:26.158
Precision asks how many reported
detections were correct.

00:09:26.238 --> 00:09:28.700
Low precision means too many false alarms.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.666
Recall asks how many real
objects the model found.

00:09:31.746 --> 00:09:34.138
Low recall means too many missed objects.

00:09:34.218 --> 00:09:36.186
F1 balances precision and recall.

00:09:36.266 --> 00:09:39.787
Mean Average Precision at an
overlap threshold of point five

00:09:39.867 --> 00:09:43.248
summarises detection quality
across confidence levels.

00:09:44.328 --> 00:09:47.261
Reco Trainer's quality
score gives seventy percent

00:09:47.341 --> 00:09:50.205
weight to mAP at point five
and thirty percent to F1.

00:09:50.285 --> 00:09:52.671
Inference time acts only as a tie-breaker.

00:09:52.751 --> 00:09:56.163
This ranking provides a useful
overview, but the deployment

00:09:56.243 --> 00:09:58.971
goal still determines
which errors matter most.

00:09:59.051 --> 00:10:02.121
A tracking system may prefer
fewer missed balls, while

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:05.339
another workflow may be more
sensitive to false alarms.

00:10:06.419 --> 00:10:09.392
Inspect per-class results as
well as the overall score.

00:10:09.472 --> 00:10:12.055
Strong performance on
common player detections

00:10:12.135 --> 00:10:14.394
can hide weak ball or referee performance.

00:10:14.474 --> 00:10:17.901
Models with different class sets
do not support a simple overall

00:10:17.981 --> 00:10:21.538
comparison, so their shared
classes deserve separate attention.

00:10:22.618 --> 00:10:26.273
Completed training runs enter an
immutable local model library.

00:10:26.353 --> 00:10:29.464
Reco Trainer can rename,
activate, compare, and delete

00:10:29.544 --> 00:10:33.198
revisions, while protecting the
active revision from deletion.

00:10:33.278 --> 00:10:36.186
A worse new run remains
available for investigation

00:10:36.266 --> 00:10:39.173
and does not silently replace
a better active model.

00:10:40.253 --> 00:10:42.744
Metrics summarise many images, but they do

00:10:42.824 --> 00:10:45.243
not show how detections behave over time.

00:10:45.323 --> 00:10:49.456
The ball-tracking simulation provides
a visual check on a short clip.

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:52.260
Green marks a direct detection.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:55.934
Orange shows an interpolated
position between real detections.

00:10:56.014 --> 00:10:59.207
Yellow holds the last known
position for a limited period.

00:10:59.287 --> 00:11:03.149
A lost state means the system can no
longer estimate the ball reliably.

00:11:03.229 --> 00:11:06.289
The look-ahead setting
controls how far the simulation

00:11:06.369 --> 00:11:08.894
may search around a gap
before interpolating.

00:11:09.974 --> 00:11:14.516
This view helps reveal flicker,
implausible jumps, and long detection gaps.

00:11:14.596 --> 00:11:18.284
It does not save new training data
and does not change the model.

00:11:18.364 --> 00:11:22.977
Treat it as a practical inspection tool
alongside the independent benchmark.

00:11:24.057 --> 00:11:26.979
When a model is ready
for deliberate exchange,

00:11:27.059 --> 00:11:30.135
Reco Trainer can create
a dot recomodel package.

00:11:30.215 --> 00:11:32.598
The package contains model weights, a

00:11:32.678 --> 00:11:35.677
manifest, checksums,
and aggregate metadata.

00:11:35.757 --> 00:11:38.678
It excludes source
videos, extracted frames,

00:11:38.758 --> 00:11:41.834
annotations, video file
names, and local paths.

00:11:42.914 --> 00:11:46.431
During import, Reco Trainer
checks the package structure and

00:11:46.511 --> 00:11:49.959
checksum, then uses PyTorch's
safe-loading mode before the

00:11:50.039 --> 00:11:53.694
weights can support training,
automatic labelling, or export.

00:11:53.774 --> 00:11:56.668
A checksum detects accidental
changes, but it does

00:11:56.748 --> 00:11:59.297
not prove that the
publisher is trustworthy.

00:11:59.377 --> 00:12:02.271
Import model packages only
from sources you trust.

00:12:03.351 --> 00:12:05.329
The complete workflow is a cycle.

00:12:05.409 --> 00:12:07.166
Select varied local footage.

00:12:07.246 --> 00:12:09.077
Review every training answer.

00:12:09.157 --> 00:12:10.694
Train on your own machine.

00:12:10.774 --> 00:12:12.899
Test on genuinely unseen material.

00:12:12.979 --> 00:12:16.132
Compare the errors that
matter for the intended use.

00:12:16.212 --> 00:12:19.734
Then collect new examples
that address those weaknesses.

00:12:20.814 --> 00:12:24.730
Reco Trainer provides the technical
structure, but the quality of the

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:29.065
result still depends on informed human
decisions and representative data.

00:12:29.145 --> 00:12:32.248
Keep backups, validate every
model, and remember that a

00:12:32.328 --> 00:12:35.635
short demonstration cannot
replace a serious evaluation.

00:12:35.715 --> 00:12:37.870
Good models begin with good decisions.
